+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Degenerate Covariance:** If the merged covariance matrix is singular (determinant near zero), the overlap integral denominator becomes zero. A guard clause is required to return zero amplitude or handle the singularity [G].
+*   **Precision:** The calculation involves sums of products of amplitudes and covariances. In high dynamic range scenes, floating-point precision errors may accumulate in the moment calculations [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Sparse Subsamples:** The method's performance relative to culling depends on data density. On sparse subsamples, mass-ranked culling may outperform this merging technique in median quality [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Analytical Merging of Gaussian Splats
-A closed-form framework for merging two Gaussian splats using L2 integrated squared-error, providing a geometric interpretation of the relative merging error [S1, unofficial]. The relative merging error is $\epsilon = \sin^2 \alpha$, where $\alpha$ is the geometric angle parameter in the error derivation [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in Luxar for merging Gaussian splats during fitting or LOD reduction [S1, unofficial].
+A closed-form framework for merging exactly two Gaussian splats using L2 integrated squared-error, providing a geometric interpretation of the relative merging error [S1, unofficial]. The relative merging error is given by $\epsilon = \sin^2 \alpha$ for the amplitude-optimised moment-matched template, where $\alpha$ is a geometric angle parameter in the error derivation [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in Luxar for merging Gaussian splats during fitting or LOD reduction [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Depends on the partitioning algorithm. Greedy hierarchical merging is $O(N^2)$ or $O(N \log N)$ with a heap. Cost-increment relocation is faster, matching greedy performance within 5.5% at compression factors $\ge 4$ [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the input and $O(M)$ for the output, where $M$ is the target splat count.
-- **Performance:** On full fits, cost-aware merging outperforms mass-ranked culling regardless of amplitude refit [S1, unofficial]. On sparse subsamples, culling wins in the median [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Provides a faithful surrogate for global error once spatial partition separates bins (single trials deviate by tens of percent at moderate separation) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of positive definiteness for covariance matrices.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The merging step itself is $O(K)$ per cluster for moment calculation and $O(K)$ for amplitude refit (assuming constant-time overlap evaluation). Partitioning dominates the preprocessing cost. Greedy hierarchical partitioning is generally $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N^2)$ depending on implementation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the cluster members and intermediate moment sums. No significant additional memory overhead compared to standard merging [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Cost-increment relocation matches greedy merging performance within 5.5% at compression factors of four and above [S1, unofficial]. On full fits, cost-aware merging outperforms mass-ranked culling regardless of amplitude refit [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides a theoretically grounded optimal amplitude that minimizes L2 error, leading to better reconstruction quality on dense data compared to heuristic merging [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** More computationally intensive than simple averaging due to the overlap integral calculations. Less effective than culling on sparse subsamples [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from the Luxar project, which provides Gaussian splatting for microscopy and scalable interactive web visualization of multidimensional scientific data [S1, unofficial]. It is described in Supplementary Document 8 of the Luxar documentation, which details the cost-metric design space for substitutive LOD problems [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the Luxar project, specifically from supplementary documents detailing substitutive levels of detail for Gaussian splats [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a broader effort to create scalable interactive web visualisation of multidimensional scientific data, particularly microscopy data [S1, unofficial]. The technique is described in the context of a cost-metric design space including L2, Cauchy–Schwarz, gKL, W2, and MMD metrics [S1, unofficial].
 
